Field & Laboratory Playbooks: Non-Destructive First

Playbook 1 — Ant highway to hidden resource

Objective: Determine whether a trail is feeding on plant nectaries, sap-feeder honeydew, fallen fruit/debris, or simply transiting to a nest.

Tools: phone camera, notebook, optional hand lens; no bait or insecticide required.

1. Observe 10 minutes at a comfortable distance without blocking the trail.
2. Sketch branching structure; note direction of food carriers and edge of building/plant.
3. Find endpoints, inspect leaf undersides for sap feeders, inspect extra-floral glands, locate fallen fruit and openings.
4. Record weather, time, shade, substrate, plant ID (uncertain IDs marked uncertain), other arthropods.
5. Return at a second time of day. Look for changes in flow or destination.
6. If ants only cross exterior siding with no entry or wood damage, leave them undisturbed. Escalate to structural inspection if wood frass, persistent indoor trails, moisture or other real signs of damage develop.
7. Use examples/ant_ambiguous.json as a template; replace synthetic data and mark actual origin. Do not claim the demonstration priors are calibrated.

Rival explanations: honeydew, extrafloral nectar, fruit, nesting, moisture, trail shortcut, aphids obscured by foliage, predator avoidance.

Avoid: dumping sugar outdoors, disturbing nests, pulling apart plant tissues, handling biting insects, assuming ant attraction makes material edible.

Playbook 2 — Maize/plant volatile grammar (research setting)

Question: Can leaf-wounding and herbivore-associated chemical patterns be differentiated under controlled conditions?

Minimum design: matched plants in same developmental stage; undamaged controls, standardized mechanical damage controls, and natural herbivory observations. Replicate at plant and day level. Collect environmental conditions and timing. Avoid introducing herbivores into the environment.

Data: visual herbivore inspection, leaf damage score, optional lab headspace collection (SPME or dynamic collection) plus GC-MS with blanks/internal standards, retention confirmation, detection limits. Root exudate profiling is a separate LC-MS-capable workflow.

Tests: pre-register potential discriminators (indole, green-leaf volatile profile, visible caterpillar, timing); separate training/calibration sites from evaluation sites; investigate false positives. Quantified chemistry requires correct units and authentic standards. Do not treat smell or insect visitation as chemical identification.

Avoid: spraying concentrated synthetic signaling chemicals near people/pets or off-label, improvising solvent extraction, claiming that herbivory is proven by a single volatile.

Playbook 3 — Root ecosystems and fungi

Question: Does a root–microbe association plausibly depend on specific exudates?

Non-invasive exploration: note host species, soil context, fungi, growth stage, disturbance history. For laboratory follow-up, compare known-root-exudate producers/controls with microbial colonization; chemical confirmation uses validated LC-MS protocols. Strigolactones are chemically diverse and labile; results depend on assay and genotype.

Rivals: moisture, background carbon source, soil pH, plant genotype, seasonal microbiome shifts. Do not dig or disturb protected habitats solely to find interesting networks.

Playbook 4 — Spider webs as passive samplers

Question: Which vertebrates and arthropods left genetic traces in a monitored region?

Process (research laboratory): obtain permission; sample abandoned/available webs without disturbing occupied active ones; use sterilized sampling controls and site blanks; perform PCR/sequencing using validated protocols; compare against reference barcode databases. Contamination, transported DNA and differential detection remain major limits. eDNA detection does not establish exact current location of a species.

Playbook 5 — Termite bioprocess as comparative morphology

Question: What division of labor makes recalcitrant plant polymer usable by a host?

Desk research / existing microscopy datasets: distinguish chewing, enzymatic hydrolysis, microbial fermentation, hydrogen recycling, acetate uptake. Compare termite lineages and fungus-growing termites. No culturing of unknown gut microbes and no termite introduction to buildings. See TERMITE_REACTOR.md.

